In article <24949@cs.yale.edu> beigel-richard@CS.YALE.EDU (Richard Beigel) writes:
>>[gilbert@BINAH.CC.BRANDEIS.EDU writes:]
>>[] If you don't know what you want you shouldn't get it.
>
>In article <HAIRSTON.90May2184913@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>>as you can tell, i don't think a feature of the game should be the
>>player's ignorance.
>
>By that token, all monster descriptions should describe their special
>attacks, defenses, AC, hit-dice, etc.  You wouldn't want a player to
>be ignorant, would you?  Of course this would take most of the
>surprises out of the game, as would giving a player a list of things
>devoutly to be wished for.  Aye, there's the rub.

True.  The one thing Nethack has tried to be is a completely regeneratable
game - you can play it over and over again and still find totally new ways
to have fun.  This is in contrast to many commercial games (eg Ultima x),
where, once you have explored the precreated area, the game is worthless.
You might call this a "discovery" game, and Nethack a "strategy" game.

But that "ignorance" idea chisels away at this ideal.  If I have to discover
all the little tricks and names of things before I can have a good game, it
takes a while before Nethack is fun.  On the other hand, if Nethack is fun at
first, but too easy once I know the spoilers, then something's also wrong.
We've made the game into a "discovery" game.

The solution?  Introduce more strategic options.  If I have more strategy
choices to make, even when I know all about what each of them is, the game
won't be boring.  Right now, I can start a game of Nethack, play a priest or a
valkyrie, and take my character down to the stronghold, surviving maybe 1/3 of
the time, on high-speed autopilot mode.  This is really tedious.  I don't play
Nethack a lot these days.  But if I actually had to think about what I was
doing... the game might be a lot more worth playing.  Surprises are still
possible when the player isn't ignorant; they're just harder to design.

>Gilbert writes (about wishes)
>>
>>[] genies don't want to give them out and give out tricked ones
>>[] if they can.  there is a famous saying "Be careful what you wish for . . .
>>[] you may get it!
>
>(3) convenient: gives you what you want be taking it from the most
>convenient source.  If you have 3 amulets of lifesaving and a potion
>of holy water, it dips one of your amulets of lifesaving in the holy
>water and then gives you your own blessed amulet.  If you wish for a
>blessed longsword named Excalibur it steals one from a hacker named
>Bonga who shows up ten turns later looking for it.

Excellent idea!  This is very similar to what wishes do in traditional
mythology.  Something needs to be done to counteract the high number
of wishes; I average about one every ten levels (through fountains
and magic lamps), and with +3 speed boots and +3 Mojo, I can kill
anything that moves, except for the Wiz and maybe a golden naga or
minotaur.

